<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shitao</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1642">1642</a>–<a href="/years/1707">1707</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Shi Tao, Yuan Ji, Zhu Ruoji, Daoji, Kugua Heshang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shitao, born Zhu Ruoji into the Ming imperial clan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanzhou">Quanzhou</a> County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxi">Guangxi</a>, in 1642, was a Chinese Buddhist monk, calligrapher and landscape painter of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>. He escaped the fall of the Ming in 1644, became a Buddhist monk no later than 1651, lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangzhou">Yangzhou</a> in the 1680s and sought patronage in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> from 1690. When no patron was found, he converted to Daoism in 1693 and returned to Yangzhou, where he died in 1707. An individualist painter, he broke with codified styles through washes, bold brushstrokes and blank space, and wrote Sayings on Painting from Monk Bitter Gourd.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shitao.jpg" alt="Shitao" width="330" height="364" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shitao (石濤) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shitao-autoportrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
